{"cluster_id": "C-2888", "topic": "IDataPartStorage::precommitTransaction is called before final part", "target": "architecture/part-lifecycle", "member_ids": ["B041-119"], "canonical_claim": "IDataPartStorage::precommitTransaction is called before final part rename and therefore is not automatically a safe externally visible CAS publication boundary.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/datapartstorage-content-addressed-design-concern.md#L185-L191"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-2889", "topic": "A new CA part-storage implementation requires", "target": "architecture/part-lifecycle", "member_ids": ["B041-124", "B041-128"], "canonical_claim": "A new CA part-storage implementation requires a centralized factory because direct construction in fetch, part loading, and temporary text-index code could otherwise create two part-storage semantics on one disk.; The proposed spike should centralize DataPartStorageOnDiskFull construction, add a skeletal DataPartStorageContentAddressed for CA disks, implement one typed committed read and transactional write, test temporary-to-final rename and projection transaction sharing, and compare typed and path-derived routes.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/datapartstorage-content-addressed-design-concern.md#L272-L286", "docs/superpowers/cas/datapartstorage-content-addressed-design-concern.md#L325-L346"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-2890", "topic": "A CA tryCommit retry can lose", "target": "architecture/part-lifecycle", "member_ids": ["B043-068"], "canonical_claim": "A CA `tryCommit` retry can lose staged `writeFile` or `createHardLink` state because the reset `metadata_transaction` has no `operations_to_execute` entry to refill the in-memory staging maps.", "issue_ids": ["B82"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/random_notes.md#lines-355-355"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-2891", "topic": "Build::abandon retires build_seq before appending the", "target": "architecture/part-lifecycle", "member_ids": ["B035-035", "B044-003"], "canonical_claim": "`Build::abandon` retires `build_seq` before appending the precommit-removal event, allowing GC precommit reclamation to race the abandonment removal.; `Build::abandon` must call `retireBuildSeq` only after the precommit-removal `mutateShard`, so the build becomes watermark-dead only after its removal is durable; this ordering mirrors `Build::promote`.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/reviews.md#L40-L43", "utils/ca-soak/scenarios/BACKLOG.md#L1368-L1379"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-2892", "topic": "The clone/freeze path could perform per-file", "target": "architecture/part-lifecycle", "member_ids": ["B051-009"], "canonical_claim": "The clone/freeze path could perform per-file operations without an enclosing whole-part transaction, so a logical part clone was not represented as one disk transaction.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/upstream.md#L33-L38"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-2893", "topic": "On plain S3, mutation contract", "target": "architecture/part-lifecycle", "member_ids": ["B051-045", "B051-057"], "canonical_claim": "On plain S3, mutation finalization can make `loadProjections` see only committed metadata, silently skip a carried-forward projection, and leave the in-memory mutated part without that projection until reload. `createHardLink` must stage the hardlink eagerly so `loadProjections` during the same finalization can see a carried-forward projection.", "issue_ids": ["B58", "B63"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/upstream-patch-inventory.md#disk-object-storage-transaction-cpp", "docs/superpowers/cas/upstream.md#L334-L342"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-2894", "topic": "D8 (mostly duplicated): the frontend implements", "target": "architecture/part-lifecycle", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-236"], "canonical_claim": "D8 (mostly duplicated): the frontend implements `existsDirectory` vs `listDirectory` path-shape routing twice with the same fixed dispatch order (`shadow -> atomic-shard -> table-uuid -> part -> subdir -> generic`), which must be kept in sync manually between the two implementations \u2014 the highest-value consolidation target after D9 in the author's own assessment.", "issue_ids": ["D8"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-12-cas-archaeology/00-REPORT.md#L529"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-2895", "topic": "B188 replaced an eager HEAD of", "target": "architecture/part-lifecycle", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-324"], "canonical_claim": "B188 replaced an eager HEAD of a committed source with `adoptEvidence` and made precommit-first ordering gate reads as well as writes.", "issue_ids": ["B188"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-12-cas-archaeology/06-tests.md#3-regression-pin-archaeology-bug-ids-invariant-encoded"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-2896", "topic": "A rebirth test using an Atomic", "target": "architecture/part-lifecycle", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-350", "B031-036"], "canonical_claim": "A rebirth test using an `Atomic` database fixture silently gets a fresh UUID and never actually tests same-name rebirth; a valid rebirth test must pre-install a nonzero predecessor cursor, reuse the writer epoch, prove the same `RootNamespace` was reused, and interleave a post-prune GC round.; Normal `Atomic` recreation creates a fresh table UUID and therefore a fresh namespace; it does not wait for the prior namespace to be removed.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-31-stage-b-strategic-review-codex-round2.md#round-2-conclusion", "docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-31-task5-critique-codex.md#test-by-test-would-it-fail-on-regression"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-2897", "topic": "ContentAddressedTransaction::commit compensates for a mid-commit exception", "target": "architecture/part-lifecycle", "member_ids": ["B001-067"], "canonical_claim": "ContentAddressedTransaction::commit compensates for a mid-commit exception by best-effort dropping newly created refs and then rethrows.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-12-cas-archaeology/01-frontend.md#L168-L180"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-2898", "topic": "moveDirectory publishes a CAS rename eagerly", "target": "architecture/part-lifecycle", "member_ids": ["B001-069"], "canonical_claim": "moveDirectory publishes a CAS rename eagerly, outside the later transaction commit, to avoid holding MergeTree locks during object-store I/O.", "issue_ids": ["B151"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-12-cas-archaeology/01-frontend.md#L232-L241"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-2899", "topic": "CAS exposes supportsTransactionalMutableFiles separately contract", "target": "architecture/part-lifecycle", "member_ids": ["B001-077", "B051-098"], "canonical_claim": "CAS exposes supportsTransactionalMutableFiles separately from append support because transactions rewrite txn_version.txt and do not require append. `StorageMergeTree::supportTransaction` accepts a disk that reports `supportsTransactionalMutableFiles()` even when the disk does not support append, allowing CAS to persist per-part MVCC `txn_version.txt` in its per-ref sidecar.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/03-writer-protocol.md#L542-L559", "docs/superpowers/cas/upstream-patch-inventory.md#storage-merge-tree-cpp"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-2900", "topic": "A new_binding event contributes +1 for", "target": "architecture/part-lifecycle", "member_ids": ["B002-016"], "canonical_claim": "A `new_binding` event contributes `+1` for each manifest blob, a true `old_binding` removal contributes `\u22121`, an equal old/new binding contributes zero, and a repoint contributes `\u22121` for the old manifest plus `+1` for the new manifest.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/04-gc-protocol.md#fold"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-2901", "topic": "A missing precommit body is non-activating", "target": "architecture/part-lifecycle", "member_ids": ["B002-018"], "canonical_claim": "A missing precommit body is non-activating; when the body appears, the fold activates it and emits its `+1` deltas, while an abandoned precommit emits no deltas.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/04-gc-protocol.md#fold"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-2902", "topic": "Part manifests are deleted only after", "target": "architecture/part-lifecycle", "member_ids": ["B002-039"], "canonical_claim": "Part manifests are deleted only after the adopted fold has sealed their blob decrements, and pre-precommit debris is handled by a bounded exact-token orphan sweep.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/04-gc-protocol.md#manifest-cleanup"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-2903", "topic": "A Cas::Build is never persisted and", "target": "architecture/part-lifecycle", "member_ids": ["B002-066"], "canonical_claim": "A `Cas::Build` is never persisted and has no precommit replay path; GC never invents a ref transition or removes a dead precommit.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-12-cas-archaeology/04-ops-mounts.md#3-2-casbuild-build-writer-path-not-rebuild-public-methods"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-2904", "topic": "A CA MergeTree part contract", "target": "architecture/part-lifecycle", "member_ids": ["B002-119", "B041-113", "B051-010", "B051-083"], "canonical_claim": "A CA MergeTree part is modeled as many files to one manifest to one ref, so `freeze()` creates one whole-part `DiskTransaction` when the caller supplies none. When the caller does not provide a transaction, `src/Storages/MergeTree/DataPartStorageOnDiskBase.cpp:514` creates a whole-part transaction for cloning, and `metadata_version.txt` is written inside that transaction at line 553. `DataPartStorageOnDiskBase::freeze` wraps the whole clone in an owned transaction and writes `metadata_version.txt` inside it, preserving whole-part atomicity from many files to one manifest and one ref.; CAS should keep the logical part format Full; it changes physical representation and publication protocol, not the independent MergeTree files such as checksums.txt, columns, marks, projections, and metadata.", "issue_ids": ["B21", "B34", "B36"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/datapartstorage-content-addressed-design-concern.md#L83-L98", "docs/superpowers/cas/upstream-patch-inventory.md#data-part-storage-on-disk-base", "docs/superpowers/cas/upstream.md#L33-L38", "docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-12-cas-archaeology/05-integration.md#mergetree-layer"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-2905", "topic": "A tree reference may be published", "target": "architecture/part-lifecycle", "member_ids": ["B002-162"], "canonical_claim": "A tree reference may be published only when all direct children are present and non-condemned at publish time.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/06-tla-models.md#caincarnationcore"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-2906", "topic": "A root ref may own at", "target": "architecture/part-lifecycle", "member_ids": ["B002-182"], "canonical_claim": "A root ref may own at most one committed manifest; `Build::promote` must abort on a different existing committed manifest, while same-content republish is idempotent.", "issue_ids": ["PROMOTE-OVER-COMMITTED-LEAK"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/06-tla-models.md#atmostonecommittedmanifestperref"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-2907", "topic": "The edge-before-observe model preserves contract", "target": "architecture/part-lifecycle", "member_ids": ["B002-186", "B037-113", "B043-020"], "canonical_claim": "The edge-before-observe model preserves the precommit \u2192 adopt/observe \u2192 promote order and K1 condemned-meta adoption gate; tokened leaves need no promote-time revalidation in the current code. With durable precommit closure before adopt/observe and same-pass GC recheck, promote-time revalidation of already-tokened leaves is redundant; dedup adoption, tokenless-leaf presence HEAD, condemned checks, and operation order remain load-bearing.; The `EDGE-BEFORE-OBSERVE` order is precommit with durable closure, then adopt/observe, then promote; under this order promote-time revalidation of tokened leaves is redundant.", "issue_ids": ["B188"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/06-tla-models.md#caedgebeforeobserve", "docs/superpowers/cas/how-we-got-here.md#turn-edge-before-observe", "docs/superpowers/models/README.md#group-writer-meta"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-2908", "topic": "The B59 in-flight overlay provides tryGetInFlightStorageObjects", "target": "architecture/part-lifecycle", "member_ids": ["B003-109"], "canonical_claim": "The B59 in-flight overlay provides `tryGetInFlightStorageObjects`, `tryReadFileInFlight`, and `tryGetInFlightFileSize`, with inline staged bytes served by `ReadBufferFromOwnString`.", "issue_ids": ["B59"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/09-read-protocol.md#b59-overlay"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-2909", "topic": "hasInFlightDirectory(path) returns true when staged entries", "target": "architecture/part-lifecycle", "member_ids": ["B003-110"], "canonical_claim": "`hasInFlightDirectory(path)` returns true when staged entries have a `<proj>.proj/` prefix, allowing `loadProjections` to discover an in-flight projection through `existsDirectory`.", "issue_ids": ["B59"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/09-read-protocol.md#b59-directory"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-2910", "topic": "The durable part-write order contract", "target": "architecture/part-lifecycle", "member_ids": ["B004-014", "B004-064", "B009-051", "B023-098"], "canonical_claim": "The durable part-write order is `stageManifest` \u2192 `precommitAdd` \u2192 `putBlob` \u2192 `promote`, and adopting an existing blob is legal only after this build's precommit is durable. The canonical part publication sequence is `stageManifest` \u2192 `precommitAdd` \u2192 `putBlob` \u2192 `promote`; `promote` publishes no blob deltas because the manifest retains an owner. The write path is stageManifest \u2192 precommitAdd \u2192 putBlob \u2192 promote, and the precommit-to-blob edge protects a new blob only after GC folds the create-precommit event with the manifest body. The intra-part order `stageManifest \u2192 precommitAdd \u2192 uploadPendingBlobs \u2192 promote` is an invariant corresponding to EDGE-BEFORE-OBSERVE and must not change under parallelization.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/11-walkthrough.md#part-add", "docs/superpowers/cas/11-walkthrough.md#safety-invariants", "docs/superpowers/plans/2026-07-22-cas-parallel-write-path.md#global-constraints", "docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-09-cas-promote-resurrect-tokened-blob-design.md##root-cause"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-2911", "topic": "PartWriteTxn is in-memory only and is", "target": "architecture/part-lifecycle", "member_ids": ["B004-020"], "canonical_claim": "`PartWriteTxn` is in-memory only and is never resumed after restart; a dead precommit is removed rather than promoted.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/11-walkthrough.md#safety-invariants"]}
